        "When unexpected external forces push an articulating joint into an extreme passive position where your nervous system cannot fire motor units, passive connective tissues (such as ligaments, fibrocartilage labrums, joint capsules, and tendons) absorb the entire mechanical load without muscular deceleration, culminating in tears, labral fraying, and chronic impingements. Building active neurological control across your outer end-ranges turns passive flexibility into functional athletic armor that protects against sudden twisting forces, heavy barbell misgrooves, and everyday slips.",
